\title{Infinitesimal Point Masses on the Finite--Cofinite Algebra\\
\large A Normalized Surreal-Valued Probability on Arbitrary Infinite Sample Spaces}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 2, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We construct a normalized, nonnegative, finitely additive probability with values in the surreal numbers on the finite--cofinite Boolean algebra of any infinite sample space. The construction begins with the Conway cut
\[
\varepsilon=\{0\mid 1,2^{-1},2^{-2},\ldots\},
\]
which is strictly positive and smaller than $2^{-n}$ for every natural number $n$. A finite event $A$ receives mass $|A|\varepsilon$, while a cofinite event receives mass $1-|A^c|\varepsilon$. We prove normalization, equal positive infinitesimal singleton masses, finite additivity, the complement identity, unit bounds, monotonicity, a relative-difference formula, and strict growth by exactly $\varepsilon$ when a new point is adjoined to a finite event. Specializing to $[0,1]$ gives equal nonzero infinitesimal point probabilities while retaining total mass one, on the explicitly stated event algebra and under finite additivity.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

In an atomless probability distribution on $[0,1]$, every singleton has probability zero. This is compatible with total mass one because countable additivity does not express a continuum as a countable disjoint union of its points. A different question is whether every point can receive the same strictly positive mass, smaller than every ordinary positive dyadic scale, while the whole interval retains probability one.

Surreal numbers contain positive infinitesimals and therefore provide candidate values. Changing the codomain alone is insufficient: the event algebra and additivity principle must be coordinated with the point assignment. We use the finite--cofinite algebra, the smallest natural Boolean algebra containing every singleton. Its members are controlled by finitely many included points or finitely many excluded points.

For an infinite sample space $\Omega$, our probability is
\[
P(A)=
\begin{cases}
|A|\varepsilon,&A\text{ finite},\\
1-|A^c|\varepsilon,&A^c\text{ finite}.
\end{cases}
\]
The construction is not an extension of Lebesgue measure and does not assert countable additivity. It is a coherent model designed to isolate the compatibility of normalization, finite additivity, and positive infinitesimal point masses.

\section{The dyadic surreal infinitesimal}

A Conway cut $\{L\mid R\}$ denotes the simplest surreal number lying above every left option and below every right option, provided all left options are below all right options.

\begin{definition}[Dyadic infinitesimal]
Define
\[
\varepsilon=\{0\mid 1,1/2,1/4,1/8,\ldots\}
=\{0\mid 2^{-n}:n\in\mathbb N\}.
\]
The cut is valid because $0<2^{-n}$ for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Positivity and infinitesimal bounds]
The number $\varepsilon$ satisfies $0<\varepsilon$ and
\[
\varepsilon<2^{-n}
\]
for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The left option $0$ lies below the number represented by the cut, and every right option $2^{-n}$ lies above it. These are precisely the order properties of the defining Conway cut.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Finite multiples remain below one]
For every $k\in\mathbb N$,
\[
k\varepsilon<1.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The elementary inequality $k\le 2^k$ holds for all natural numbers. Positivity gives
\[
k\varepsilon\le 2^k\varepsilon.
\]
Since $\varepsilon<2^{-k}$, multiplication by the positive integer $2^k$ yields
\[
2^k\varepsilon<2^k2^{-k}=1.
\]
Combining the inequalities proves the result.
\end{proof}

This is the essential non-Archimedean estimate: no finite collection of point masses exhausts total probability.

\section{The event algebra}

\begin{definition}[Finite--cofinite event]
Let $\Omega$ be an infinite set. A subset $A\subseteq\Omega$ is a finite--cofinite event when either $A$ is finite or
\[
A^c=\Omega\setminus A
\]
is finite. The collection of all such events is denoted $\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Boolean closure]
The collection $\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$ contains $\varnothing$ and $\Omega$ and is closed under complements, finite unions, finite intersections, and relative differences.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The empty set is finite, and the complement of $\Omega$ is empty. Complementation exchanges finite and cofinite events. If $A$ and $B$ are finite, then $A\cup B$ is finite. If at least one is cofinite, then
\[
(A\cup B)^c=A^c\cap B^c
\]
is a subset of a finite set. Intersections follow from De Morgan's law, and relative differences from $A\setminus B=A\cap B^c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exclusivity]
No subset of an infinite $\Omega$ is both finite and cofinite.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If both $A$ and $A^c$ were finite, then $\Omega=A\cup A^c$ would be finite, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Construction and elementary properties}

\begin{definition}[Surreal finite--cofinite probability]
For $A\in\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$, define
\[
P(A)=
\begin{cases}
|A|\varepsilon,&A\text{ is finite},\\[3pt]
1-|A^c|\varepsilon,&A^c\text{ is finite}.
\end{cases}
\]
The preceding lemma makes the definition unambiguous.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Normalization]
The empty and certain events satisfy
\[
P(\varnothing)=0,
\qquad
P(\Omega)=1.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The empty set has cardinality zero, and the complement of $\Omega$ is empty. Substitution in the two clauses of the definition gives the identities.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Equal infinitesimal singleton masses]
For every $x\in\Omega$,
\[
P(\{x\})=\varepsilon,
\]
and hence, for every $n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
0<P(\{x\})<2^{-n}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A singleton has cardinality one. The formula and inequalities follow from the definition and the bounds on $\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Nonnegativity]
For every $A\in\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$,
\[
P(A)\ge0.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $A$ is finite, $P(A)=|A|\varepsilon\ge0$. If $A$ is cofinite, the finite-multiple lemma gives $|A^c|\varepsilon<1$, so $P(A)=1-|A^c|\varepsilon>0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Finite additivity}

\begin{theorem}[Binary finite additivity]
If $A,B\in\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$ are disjoint, then
\[
P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose first that both events are finite. Disjointness gives $|A\cup B|=|A|+|B|$, and therefore
\[
P(A\cup B)=(|A|+|B|)\varepsilon=P(A)+P(B).
\]

Next suppose that $A$ is finite and $B$ is cofinite. Disjointness gives $A\subseteq B^c$ and the disjoint decomposition
\[
B^c=A\sqcup(A^c\cap B^c).
\]
Because $(A\cup B)^c=A^c\cap B^c$, cardinalities imply
\[
|B^c|=|A|+|(A\cup B)^c|.
\]
Consequently,
\begin{align*}
P(A)+P(B)
&=|A|\varepsilon+1-|B^c|\varepsilon\\
&=1-|(A\cup B)^c|\varepsilon\\
&=P(A\cup B).
\end{align*}
The case in which $A$ is cofinite and $B$ finite is symmetric.

Finally, two cofinite events cannot be disjoint. Otherwise $A\cap B=\varnothing$ would imply $A^c\cup B^c=\Omega$, expressing the infinite set $\Omega$ as a finite union of finite sets. Thus all possible cases satisfy the identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite--cofinite surreal probability space]
The map $P$ is a normalized, nonnegative, finitely additive surreal-valued probability on the Boolean algebra $\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Order and difference laws}

\begin{theorem}[Complement law]
For every $A\in\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$,
\[
P(A^c)=1-P(A).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The events $A$ and $A^c$ are disjoint and have union $\Omega$. Finite additivity and normalization give $P(A)+P(A^c)=1$, which rearranges to the stated identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unit bounds]
Every event satisfies
\[
0\le P(A)\le1.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The lower bound is nonnegativity. Applying nonnegativity to $A^c$ and using the complement law gives $0\le1-P(A)$, hence the upper bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]
If $A,B\in\mathcal F_{\mathrm{fc}}(\Omega)$ and $A\subseteq B$, then
\[
P(A)\le P(B).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The relative difference $B\setminus A$ is an event, and
\[
B=A\sqcup(B\setminus A).
\]
Thus $P(B)=P(A)+P(B\setminus A)$. The second term is nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Relative-difference formula]
If $B\subseteq A$, then
\[
P(A\setminus B)=P(A)-P(B).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use the disjoint decomposition $A=B\sqcup(A\setminus B)$, apply finite additivity, and rearrange.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict increment under point insertion]
Let $A$ be finite and let $x\in\Omega\setminus A$. Then
\[
P(A\cup\{x\})=P(A)+\varepsilon
\]
and
\[
P(A)<P(A\cup\{x\}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $x\notin A$, one has $|A\cup\{x\}|=|A|+1$. Hence
\[
P(A\cup\{x\})=(|A|+1)\varepsilon=P(A)+\varepsilon.
\]
Strictness follows from $\varepsilon>0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The unit interval}

\begin{theorem}[Infinitesimal point probability on the unit interval]
On the finite--cofinite Boolean algebra of $[0,1]$, there exists a surreal-valued probability $P$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $P([0,1])=1$;
\item $P(\{x\})=\varepsilon>0$ for every $x\in[0,1]$;
\item $P(\{x\})<2^{-n}$ for every $x\in[0,1]$ and $n\in\mathbb N$;
\item $P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)$ for disjoint finite--cofinite events;
\item $0\le P(A)\le1$ for every event;
\item $A\subseteq B$ implies $P(A)\le P(B)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The interval $[0,1]$ is infinite. Apply the construction and the preceding normalization, singleton, additivity, bounds, and monotonicity results.
\end{proof}

This theorem gives a precise positive answer to the existence question under explicit structural conditions. A typical subinterval such as $[0,1/2]$ is not finite or cofinite and hence is not an event in this model. No countable-additivity assertion is made.

\section{Algorithms and finite representations}

Each event admits a finite representation by a mode and a finite set $S$. In finite mode, $S$ denotes itself; in cofinite mode, it denotes the event $\Omega\setminus S$. A probability is represented exactly by a coefficient pair $(a,b)$ denoting $a+b\varepsilon$.

\begin{algorithm}[Symbolic probability evaluation]
Given a mode and finite exception set $S$, return $(0,|S|)$ in finite mode and $(1,-|S|)$ in cofinite mode. If $S$ is held in a hash set, constructing the representation takes expected $O(|S|)$ time and $O(|S|)$ memory. Reading a stored cardinality and forming the coefficient pair take constant time.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Disjoint-union audit]
For finite representations $S$ and $T$, proceed as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If both events are finite, test $S\cap T=\varnothing$.
\item If $S$ represents a finite event and $T$ the complement of a cofinite event, test $S\subseteq T$.
\item Reverse the preceding test in the symmetric mixed case.
\item Reject the possibility that two cofinite events are disjoint on an infinite space.
\item Construct the union representation and compare the coefficient pair of its probability with the sum of the two input pairs.
\end{enumerate}
With hash sets, the required set operations take expected $O(|S|+|T|)$ time in the worst mixed construction and linear memory in the finite input size.
\end{algorithm}

A floating-point approximation cannot represent $\varepsilon$: every positive floating-point value is an ordinary positive real and eventually exceeds some $2^{-n}$. Numerical illustrations may choose a finite depth $N$ and use $2^{-(N+1)}$ as a proxy below the first $N+1$ dyadic scales, but exact computation should retain the symbolic pair.

\section{Interpretation and limitations}

The construction depends on three matched choices. The surreal codomain supplies a positive value below every positive dyadic real. The finite--cofinite algebra ensures that every event is controlled by finite cardinality data. Finite additivity requires no notion of convergence for an infinite sum of copies of $\varepsilon$.

The image of $P$ is especially simple: every value is either $n\varepsilon$ or $1-n\varepsilon$ for some $n\in\mathbb N$. The former values occupy an infinitesimal neighborhood of zero and the latter an infinitesimal neighborhood of one. This limited image reflects the limited event algebra. The model detects finite exceptions but not geometry, length, or density.

It is also permutation invariant. Any bijection of $\Omega$ preserves finite cardinalities and complementary finite cardinalities, so
\[
P(f(A))=P(A)
\]
for every bijection $f:\Omega\to\Omega$ and every finite--cofinite event $A$. In this precise sense, the assignment treats all points uniformly.

The theory must not be conflated with Lebesgue probability. Lebesgue probability measures subintervals by length and gives singleton mass zero. Here most nontrivial subintervals are outside the event algebra, while singleton masses remain visible. Nor has the series
\[
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\varepsilon
\]
been assigned a value. Any countable-additivity theorem or obstruction requires an explicit convergence theory for surreal-valued series.

\section{Future directions}

First, binary finite additivity should extend by induction to finite pairwise-disjoint families:
\[
P\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}A_i\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}P(A_i).
\]
Second, strict monotonicity should be classified by proving, for $A\subseteq B$,
\[
P(A)<P(B)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad A\ne B.
\]
Third, the usual inclusion--exclusion identity is expected:
\[
P(A\cup B)+P(A\cap B)=P(A)+P(B).
\]
Fourth, a specified convergence structure may permit a rigorous obstruction to countably additive extensions with equal positive singleton masses on a countably infinite subset. Finally, one may seek a proper Boolean algebra extending the finite--cofinite algebra on $[0,1]$ while preserving normalization, positivity, finite additivity, and singleton mass $\varepsilon$, or prove that a natural candidate algebra admits no such extension.

\section{Conclusion}

Every infinite set supports a normalized, nonnegative, finitely additive surreal-valued probability on its finite--cofinite algebra that assigns every point the same positive infinitesimal mass. The explicit cut $\varepsilon=\{0\mid1,1/2,1/4,\ldots\}$ lies below every reciprocal dyadic scale, and finite events receive $|A|\varepsilon$ while cofinite events receive $1-|A^c|\varepsilon$. The resulting probability satisfies complement, order, subtraction, and strict finite-increment laws.

On $[0,1]$, every singleton consequently has a nonzero chance while the whole interval has mass one. The model demonstrates that zero point mass is not forced by normalization and finite additivity alone; it depends on further choices concerning admissible events and infinite summation.

